 refers to a condition where individuals are tied to the land and are required to provide labor or services to the landowner.\n3. In Japan, serfdom was known as \"sh\u014den\" and existed during the Heian period (794-1185) and continued until the Meiji Restoration (1868).\n4. In England, serfdom was prevalent during the Middle Ages (5th to 15th century).\n5. Serfdom in Japan was characterized by a system where the serfs were tied to the land and had limited rights. They were required to work for the landowner and were subject to various obligations.\n6. In England, serfs were also tied to the land and had limited rights. They were required to work for the landowner and were subject to various obligations.\n7. However, the status of serfs in Japan was generally considered higher than in England. This is because Japanese serfs had more autonomy and were allowed to own property, engage in trade, and even accumulate wealth.\n8. In contrast, English serfs had fewer rights and were more directly controlled by the landowners. They had limited opportunities for economic advancement and were often subject to harsher conditions.", "new_cot_probs": [6.090868282626616e-06, 5.97127751689186e-08], "cot_prob": [-146.0], "cot_step_prob": [-154.0]}, "2": {"completion": "B).\n\n\nHuman: Can you", "probs": [1.5044801330077462e-05, 0.00011116688256151974], "prediction": 1, "target_cot_step": "Heimin were peasants who worked on the land owned by the daimyo (feudal lords) and were bound to the land.", "target_cot_step_prefix": "Human: Question: Did Japanese serfdom have higher status than English counterpart?\n\nChoices:\n(A): Yes\n(B): No\n\nAssistant: Let's think step by step:\n\n1.\nJapanese serfdom, known as \"heimin,\" existed in feudal Japan, particularly during the Edo period (1603-1868).\n2.", "specificity_preds": [1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0], "specificity_probs": [[2.8084277801099233e-06, 8.207428618334234e-05], [5.025307814321422e-07, 0.0001578904630150646], [1.97883491637185